1848 was a pretty rough year for many  european rulers it saw revolutions in  places like france the german  confederation the austrian empire and  sardinia the revolution sought to  improve living conditions settle  nationalist questions and fix government  deficiencies and despite having problems  with all of these britain did not have a  revolution or even an attempt at one in  1848 which raises the question why why  was britain spared a revolution where  much of europe wasn't so lucky so to  begin despite not having a revolution  the british people shared many of the  same grievances with their government as  those across the channel  industrialization had seen the rise of  poor working conditions many were  annoyed at the lack of political  representation and in ireland and wales  there were many who felt aggrieved by  the way westminster was running things  much of this sprang from the 1832 reform  acts which notably expanded the  electorate but only to land owners and a  few others meaning most men still  couldn't vote which unsurprisingly upset  them opposition began to organize via  working men's clubs whose members called  for more rights and better conditions  leaders of these clubs along with some  members of parliament met to draft what  was called the people's charter this  pushed for the british government to  radically change the way that parliament  worked so after the charter was  published it was promptly ignored this  was mostly because doing stuff is  annoying and the current system works  well for everyone in power so why bother  tensions rose over the following decade  and the government had to deal with  riots and strikes across the country the  leader of the chartist movement fergus  o'connor was elected an mp in 1847 and  probably began to demand change in the  house of commons nothing did change  though and when news reached britain  about the revolutions abroad the  chartists saw their chance o'connor  arranged for a large march to carry a  petition to parliament which organizers  claimed had over 5 million signatures  which for a nation of 26 million people  was quite a lot the government got wind  of this and remembering what happened in  france they wanted to be sure that there  was no threat to the british government  or the crown and they knew that the  presentation to parliament was when the  danger of revolution would be at its  greatest headed by the duke of  wellington soldiers were placed all  across london just out of sight of the  planned route and thus they wouldn't be  provoking anyone but they'd be able to  respond if anything went wrong the  government also hired an extra 100 000  special constables who were voluntary  police officers whose training consisted  of his attraction go hit someone with it  interestingly among the hundred thousand  officers recruited were former prime  minister robert peel and some guy called  louis napoleon bonaparte who would later  go on to find gainful employment in  france these extra men would prove  unnecessary though since the march in  parliament would never happen the  chartists weren't planning to violently  overthrow the government and their  leaders had faced down troops before so  why didn't they march it wasn't muskets  or truncheons that stopped them it was  the rain you see rights and reformers  were important but nobody wanted to  fight for them whilst wet and with the  coming of the rain the demonstrators  just sort of went home the petition was  delivered to parliament by a couple of  chartists and was promptly ignored turns  out most of the signatures were  forgeries and the chartist movement were  so thoroughly embarrassed that it never  recovered the chance for revolution was  gone but in the end most of the chartist  demands were adopted by the british  parliament eventually i hope you enjoyed  this episode and a special thanks to my  patrons james bisonette kelly money  maker sky chappelle corsa wolff jerry  lamdin jordan longley a